2026-11-19 04:45:18 - Agent_20261119044255 - INFO - [+] Agent signaled completion: Reviewed the lxml group findings:

Query 20261119043258 (py/lxml-dangerous-calls, cwe-78/cwe-502):
- buildlibxml.py:318 — subprocess.call in the build helper (call_subprocess). This is developer-only build tooling in the sdist/build scripts, invoked with hardcoded curl/configure/tar command lists derived from build configuration, not from untrusted runtime input. Classified FP (build file, not an exploitable runtime path).
- setupinfo.py:361 — subprocess.Popen(cmd, shell=True) inside run_command, used only by setuptools setup.py to probe the local toolchain (e.g. `xml2-config`, `xslt-config`, `cython --version`). Constant command strings, no attacker-controlled data; build-script FP.

Query 20261119044103 (py/lxml-url-scheme-check, cwe-79) in src/lxml/html/clean.py:
- Line 517 (_remove_javascript_link, invoked from doc.rewrite_links at line 305 when Cleaner.javascript is enabled) — scheme sanitization relies on _substitute_whitespace + unquote_plus then regex `(?:javascript|jscript|livescript|vbscript|data|about|mocha):`. Whitespace/NUL stripping happens before unquoting? No — unquote_plus runs first, then whitespace strip, then a single-pass case-insensitive regex, so encoded/whitespace-obfuscated schemes are mostly caught; however single-pass substitution and the unquote-before-strip ordering (double-encoding like `%256a...`, or control chars outside the regex class such as \x09 handled, but `%09` decoded then stripped, so ok) leave marginal bypass room and depend on the html parser/browser normalization. Classified MR — sanitizer is defense-in-depth and only reachable from the Cleaner with javascript=True.
- Line 538 (_has_sneaky_javascript) — same whitespace-strip-then-substring-check pattern applied to CSS style attributes plus a `_looks_like_tag_content` regex backstop; can be evaded only for non-executable/interpreter-specific CSS constructs and any residual XSS requires a browser quirk. Classified MR, low severity, note that lxml's bundled html.clean module has since been deprecated/removed upstream precisely because such regex sanitizers are unreliable.

No new independent vulnerabilities were discovered outside these locations. Total 4 findings recorded (2 FP for build tooling, 2 MR for the html.clean sanitizer heuristics).
