2026-11-19 04:49:45 - Agent_20261119044255 - INFO - [+] Agent signaled completion: Reviewed all 6 py/lxml-open-nonconst results plus one adjacent location in src/lxml/ElementInclude.py.

Findings:
- TP: src/lxml/ElementInclude.py:97 (default_loader) — opens an `href` taken straight from an untrusted document's `xi:include` element (resolved via urljoin with base_url in _include), enabling arbitrary local file read / XXE-style disclosure when include() is called on untrusted XML.
- TP: src/lxml/ElementInclude.py:120 (_lxml_default_loader) — same untrusted href, plus it will urlopen() any href containing "://" (SSRF) and open local paths otherwise; this is the default loader used by _include when no loader is supplied.
- FP: src/lxml/html/_diffcommand.py:54 and :64 — CLI entry point (options.output, filename from argv); files are explicitly supplied by the local operator, and read_file() gates existence. No untrusted boundary.
- FP: src/lxml/html/html5parser.py:235 and src/lxml/html/soupparser.py:46 — public library API functions whose documented purpose is to open a caller-provided filename/URL; the path is not derived from document data. Callers that pass untrusted input are responsible. Build/API-by-design, not a library-internal taint flow.

Note: the ElementInclude cases are documented lxml behavior with max_depth recursion limits but no path restriction; the practical mitigation is to use a custom loader, which the API supports.
